s a required symbol. So we cannot remove any node whose symbol is in required_symbols. That is a hard constraint. Then the later \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" becomes redundant because if you never remove a required symbol, they always remain represented. However the last condition \"interestingness_preserved\" is about the oracle. But here we only care about required symbols. So I'll interpret: we cannot remove any node with symbol in required_symbols. Because that matches \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". Also in the output schema, we need retained and removed node IDs. In the fixtures, nodes n4 have symbol \"s1\", which is required. If we cannot remove any node with required symbol, then n4 cannot be removed. But the algorithm might still consider it? Let's test on fixture a-000: required symbols: root and s1. Nodes with s1: n1 (removable false) and n4 (removable true, depth 2). If we cannot remove any node with symbol s1, then n4 cannot be removed. But if we could remove it, then we would lose the only s1? No, n1 still has s1. So actually removing n4 would still preserve s1. But the constraint says never remove a required symbol. So we cannot remove n4 either. That would mean no removable nodes with required symbol can be removed. But then what's the point? Possibly the algorithm is to remove nodes that are not required symbols, but we also need to check if removal would remove all occurrences of a required symbol? That seems like the typical condition: you can remove a node only if its symbol is not required OR if there exists another node with that symbol elsewhere. But the wording \"never remove ... a required symbol\" is absolute. I need to check the typical Perses reduction: It removes nodes that are \"non-terminal\" (not required) and ensures that the resulting string still satisfies the property. Here the \"interestingness oracle\" is replaced by required_symbols condition. The Perses algorithm does not have a concept of \"never remove a required symbol\" but rather \"if the node's symbol is a terminal that is required, you cannot remove it\"? Actually in Perses, the grammar determines which nodes are non-terminals, but you can remove any node as long as the resulting sequence is still parseable and reduces the size. However the prompt explicitly says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So I think we must treat it as a hard rule: any node whose symbol is in required_symbols must never be removed. So even if there are duplicates, we cannot remove them. That simplifies: we skip all nodes with symbol in required_symbols. Then only consider removable nodes with symbol not required. Then check child condition and required_symbols still present (but since required symbols are never removed, they remain). So the only condition is the child condition. Let's see: In fixture a-000, required symbols: root, s1. Nodes with s1: n1 (removable false) and n4 (removable true). We cannot remove n4 because its symbol is s1. So n4 is not a candidate. Removable nodes with non-required symbols: n2 (s2, removable true), n6 (s0, removable true). n2 depth 1, n6 depth 2. n6 parent n1 (s1). Is n6 a leaf? It has no children (no node has parent == n6). So n6 can be removed if it has no retained children (it doesn't) and required symbols remain (they do). So we remove n6 first. Then n2: depth 1, parent root. Children: n4 (symbol s1, but n4 is still present because we cannot remove it). n4 is a child of n2 and is retained (since not removed). So n2 has a retained child (n4). Therefore we cannot remove n2. So removed nodes: only n6. Then removed_node_ids = [\"n6\"], retained = all others sorted. That seems plausible.\n\nNow check fixture a-001: similar, n6 depth 2 removable, n2 depth 1 removable. n6 can be removed (leaf, not required symbol). n2 has child n4 (symbol s1, not removable? n4 is removable true but cannot be removed due to required symbol, so retained), so n2 cannot be removed. So again only n6 removed.\n\nFixture a-002: same pattern. So output would be trivial. But maybe other cases have more complexity. However the problem seems to be designed for that. I'll implement with the absolute rule: never remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols.\n\nBut note: the root symbol is \"root\", which is in required_symbols list? In fixtures, required_symbols always include \"root\". The root node's symbol is \"root\", so root cannot be removed anyway (also due to removable false). So fine.\n\nImplementation details: We need to parse case. The case may have other fields, but we only use nodes and required_symbols. We'll build a map id->node, and also a set of children for each parent.\n\nProcessing order: get all node ids, filter to removable=True and symbol not in required_symbols. Sort by depth descending, then by id. We'll maintain removed set (set of ids). For each candidate id in that order, we check:\n- If it is already removed (should not happen if we process in order).\n- Get its children from precomputed children map (we can build dict parent->list of children).\n- If any child id is not in removed set (i.e., still retained), skip.\n- Else, we simulate removal: after removal, all required symbols still present? Since we never remove required symbols, this is automatically satisfied, but to be safe we can check: ensure that after removal, for each req sym, there exists some node id not removed with that symbol. Since we only remove non-required symbols, the only way to lose a required symbol is if we remove the only node with that symbol? But we don't remove required symbols, so it's safe. But if there is a required symbol that only appears on a node that is removable? But we never remove required symbols, so it's fine. Still, we can check for completeness.\n- If both conditions pass, add to removed list, add to removed set.\n\nAfter loop, retained_node_ids = [id for id in all ids if id not in removed_set]; sort it. removed_node_ids = list in order of removal (the order we added). Return dict.\n\nEdge cases: What if a node has children that are also removable and later removed? Since we process deeper first, children are considered before parent. So when we evaluate parent, children might have been removed already. That's fine. The child condition: if any child is still retained (not removed), cannot remove parent. If all children have been removed, then parent can be removed if it meets other conditions. That matches \"never remove a node with retained children\".\n\nAlso note: there might be nodes that are not removable (removable=false) - these are always retained. They are not candidates.\n\nReturn sorted ids: \"sorted\" means alphabetical? The examples: \"retained_node_ids\" sorted? The instruction: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So we sort retained_node_ids. Typically alphabetical string sort. We'll do sorted().\n\nNow write solve(case):\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    # Build maps\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children_map = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        parent = n['parent']\n        if parent is not None:\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(n['id'])\n    all_ids = [n['id'] for n in nodes]\n    # Candidates: removable and symbol not required\n    removable = [n for n in nodes if n['removable'] and n['symbol'] not in required_symbols]\n    # Sort by depth descending, then id\n    removable.sort(key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    removed_set = set()\n    removed_order = []  # list in order\n    for node in removable:\n        nid = node['id']\n        if nid in removed_set:\n            continue\n        # Check children: any child still retained?\n        children = children_map.get(nid, [])\n        retained_children = [c for c in children if c not in removed_set]\n        if retained_children:\n            continue\n        # Check required symbols still present after removal\n        # Since we only remove non-required symbols, we just need to ensure that no required symbol is lost.\n        # But we can compute: for each req sym, check if there is a node not removed and not this node that has that symbol.\n        # However, since we never remove required symbols, it's guaranteed, but be safe.\n        # Actually, a node with required symbol might be removable? But our candidate filter excludes those.\n        # So after removal, all required symbols still present because we never remove them.\n        # But we should still check for completeness.\n        # For each required sym, check if any node (including this one? before removal) has that sym and is not removed and not this node.\n        # If this node's symbol is required, it wouldn't be in candidates.\n        # So just check: for sym in required_symbols: exists node with symbol == sym and node id not removed and node id != nid.\n        # But this node's symbol is not required, so it doesn't affect required symbols. So condition is automatically true.\n        # Still implement for safety:\n        required_ok = True\n        for sym in required_symbols:\n            # find any node with that symbol not removed\n            found = False\n            for n2 in nodes:\n                if n2['id'] != nid and n2['id'] not in removed_set and n2['symbol'] == sym:\n                    found = True\n                    break\n            if not found:\n                required_ok = False\n                break\n        if not required_ok:\n            continue\n        # Remove\n        removed_set.add(nid)\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n    retained = sorted([nid for nid in all_ids if nid not in removed_set])\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed_order, 'retained_node_ids': retained}\n\nThat should work. But note: The child condition: \"never remove a node with retained children\" means if node has any child that is retained (not removed), we cannot remove it. That's what we check. But also: \"retained children\" might include children that are not removable? That's fine.\n\nNow we need to output as JSON with key \"implementation\". The entire response must be exactly one JSON object with that key. So we'll produce {\\n \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ... \" \\n}. But careful: the Python code must be correctly escaped as a JSON string. We'll write it in a Python script to generate the JSON? But the response must be plain text (the JSON). Since I'm an AI, I need to output the final JSON directly. I'll write the Python code as a string with proper escaping. I'll use triple-quoted string inside the JSON? Actually JSON strings can contain single quotes, but it's safer to use double quotes. I'll escape newlines as \\n and backslashes as \\\\ etc. I'll write the code in a single line with \\n, but to make it readable, I can keep actual newlines in the JSON string? JSON allows literal newlines in strings? No, JSON strings cannot contain unescaped newlines. So we must escape newlines as \\n. So I'll produce a JSON object like { \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    ...\" }\n\nI'll ensure no extra spaces. Also the code must be exactly syntactically correct.\n\nI'll write the code in a compact but readable way. Use double backslashes for inner quotes? Actually inside the JSON string, we need to escape backslashes and quotes. For example, in Python code we have case['nodes'], which contains single quotes. In JSON string, single quotes are allowed (they don't need escaping). But to be safe, we can use double quotes for Python strings inside? But convention uses single quotes. It's fine. For apostrophe, no need to escape in JSON string because JSON only requires escaping of double quote, backslash, and control characters. Single quote is fine. So we can keep single quotes as is. But if we have a backslash in Python code (like for